virglrenderer: VideoToolbox sessions start and end on the decoder's queue; one time limit per picture

What: vrend's thread is QEMU's GPU thread: every guest context, the display and
their fences wait while it waits. It no longer makes any VideoToolbox session
call or waits for a decoder's queue:
- A decoder's session is made, changed (a new format: kept when VideoToolbox
  can take it, else a new one; always a new one at a VP9 key frame or an AV1
  sequence header), made again after it broke, and ended on the decoder's own
  serial queue (dec_q), in order with its pictures. vrend's thread works out
  the format description and the pixel format (P010 for 10-bit) and sends them
  with the pictures. "hardware decoder: yes" is logged there as before.
- A decoder the guest destroys stays on the list (dying) while its last
  pictures go in as they come; once none is left, its session ends on its
  queue (WaitForAsynchronousFrames, Invalidate), which then frees it:
  VideoToolbox's output callback has the codec until Invalidate returns. Its
  slot (the 48-decoder limit) counts until then.
- What the Mac decodes and encodes (VTIsHardwareDecodeSupported, the encoder
  check that makes a VTCompressionSession) is asked once, on a queue that
  virgl_video_init starts; the caps wait for it only if the guest asks before
  it answered, 10 s at most. That queue also makes and ends one small VP9
  session, so the Mac's video decoder service is connected before the guest's
  first video (the first session of a process took 230-390 ms on the mini,
  later ones 10-50 ms); qemu.log: "the Mac's video decoder service answered
  in N ms".
- Up to 16 pictures per decoder on their way (was 4): a stream's first
  pictures wait on the queue while its session starts, and none is given up
  for that alone.
One time limit for a decoded picture, OMACVM_VIDEO_GIVEUP_MS (250 ms; 50-500):
a picture not in that long after its END_FRAME is given up by vrend's poll; a
wait for it (the guest reads or writes its planes, or 4 pictures are in flight)
waits that long at most, and END_FRAME does not wait at all for one still on
the queue (its session is starting; with 16 on their way the oldest is given
up). The sync thread waits for a fence's
pictures until the last of them runs out of time (at most the limit + 100 ms)
and then marks the ones not in dropped: they are never written later, so planes
the guest uses again once the fence is out stay as the guest left them. A wait
because the guest touches a plane gives back only the pictures on their way
into that plane (and the older ones of their decoders), not every decoder's.
A session that takes the limit or longer to start is logged (at most every
10 s): "vt-video: the Mac's video decoder took 1.6 s to start (H.264 320x180):
3 pictures skipped". The queue takes up to 64 pictures not handed over yet
(was 8): each is decoded once its session is there, given up or not, since a
later picture may refer to it. OMACVM_VIDEO_DECODE_SYNC=1: no queue, all of it
on vrend's thread and END_FRAME waits, as before.
Test hooks: OMACVM_TEST_VIDEO_CREATE_DELAY_MS=N (each session start waits N ms
first), OMACVM_TEST_VIDEO_BREAK=N (picture N finds its session broken).
Why: the user's VM froze on 2026-10-28 (the guest's video daemon missed its
10 s watchdog twice, "no word from the Mac for 15 s"). On the Mac mini at
critical memory pressure, QEMU's main loop stood 1586 ms in
VTDecompressionSessionCreate (the XPC look-up of the media engine's service),
13 starts of 20 ms or more in a call-like load, while every fence of every
context waited behind it; tracks/vm-stall.md. With the queue, END_FRAME took at
most 0.08 ms while a session took 1.6 s to start, and another context's fence
signalled after 250 ms (on main: END_FRAME 25-35 ms at every session start, a
lost picture held the fences 1 s).
Applies on top of virgl-videotoolbox-decode-async.patch and every virgl patch
of the runtime before it (last in the list).
Upstream: OmacVM's own backend. Not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-video-decode-async.c (new: a session that takes 1.6 s
to start: END_FRAME under 10 ms, another context's fence within the limit +
50 ms, the given-up pictures never land later, the decoder decodes afterwards;
a decoder and a buffer destroyed while the session starts; a broken session
made again on the queue without END_FRAME waiting; an H.264 SPS change and a
VP9 resolution change sent back to back keep their order, VP9 bit for bit as
FFmpeg, profile 2 (P010) too; a lost picture's fence after 250 ms), polled,
sync-thread fences and OMACVM_VIDEO_DECODE_SYNC=1, and with AddressSanitizer;
test-video-decode.c (the 48-decoder limit: room once the closed decoder's
session ended, 10 ms), test-video-padding.c and test-video-encode.c unchanged.

